WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
So in 2008, there was this tiny, incredibly wealthy resort

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.000
island off the coast of Massachusetts, and they figured

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.400
out this paperwork loophole.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.800
A massive loophole, yeah.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.420
And it legally siphoned hundreds of millions of

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.960
healthcare dollars from the rest of the country.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.040
It was just this audacious piece of regulatory engineering.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:21.880
Truly.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.500
And today, we're looking at how a team of strategists

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.240
is trying to use that exact same loophole, like step

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:32.440
to save the medical system of Puerto Rico from just total

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.280
collapse.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.160
Right, because it is, I mean, it's the ultimate example

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.840
of fighting a war using administrative law.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:41.580
There are no television ads for this, no public debates.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.400
It's just a high stakes, multimillion dollar battle

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.000
fought entirely in the hidden plumbing

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:48.840
of the federal government.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.320
The hidden plumbing, exactly.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.080
And we get to look under the hood today for this

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.880
deep dive because we got our hands on this fascinating

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.640
stack of internal strategy documents.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:00:59.640
The war plan.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.480
Yeah.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.640
There's literally a memo in here called the FY 2028

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.680
wage index campaign war plan.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.440
And alongside it, there are these official data packs

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.000
and this highly detailed, just incredibly dense

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.640
legal and actuarial analysis titled The Viex Rural Floor.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:17.740
Oh, it's dense.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.520
It is, but reading through this stuff,

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.680
it doesn't feel like hospital administration.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:22.520
No.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.360
It feels like a heist movie.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.320
A bureaucratic heist.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.360
Right, where the vault is the US Treasury

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.400
and the lock is literally just a geographic definition.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.400
Which is what makes these documents so compelling.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.760
We almost never get to see the unvarnished playbook

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.200
for how a systemic crisis is managed behind closed doors.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.960
We are looking at a masterclass in how billions

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.040
of taxpayer dollars are routed through the health care

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.120
system based on these geographic legal fictions.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.400
Geographic legal fictions.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.240
I love that.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.720
So to understand the wild strategy these documents

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.760
propose, we first need to understand the absolute panic

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.080
that is driving it right now.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.400
The cliff.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.600
According to this war plan, on October 1st, 2027,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.760
Puerto Rico's health care system is going

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.120
to drive off a physical cliff.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.920
And when I say cliff, I mean Medicare

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.040
inpatient payments to Puerto Rico.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.760
52 hospitals are projected to fall by roughly 15.5%

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.520
overnight.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.360
Yeah, a single day revenue cut of 15.5%

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.520
for a hospital system that is already incredibly

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:22.080
fragile.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:22.960
Wow.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.080
And the root of this impending disaster

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.080
comes down to a single metric used by the federal government

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.400
called the wage index.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.960
OK, so let's unpack that metric because it's basically

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.040
the lynchpin for this entire campaign, right?

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.280
Everything revolves around it.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.680
So what exactly is a wage index

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.400
and why is it about to crater Puerto Rico's hospitals?

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.920
Well, in the Medicare system, the federal government

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.920
doesn't just pay a flat universal right

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.720
for a medical procedure.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:51.200
They adjust the payment based on local labor costs.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.080
The underlying philosophy is that it costs significantly more

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.280
to hire a registered nurse or an MRI technician in downtown

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.560
Manhattan than it does in rural Oklahoma.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.280
Right, that makes sense, cost of living.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.560
Exactly.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.160
So Medicare takes the base payment for a procedure

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:09.200
and multiplies the labor portion of it

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.520
by a geographic wage index.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.880
An index of 1.0 means your area

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.760
is exactly at the national average.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.040
So if you are at 1.2, you get a 20% bump

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.080
because your city is expensive.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:20.560
Yes.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.120
And if you were at 0.8, you get a 20% haircut.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:24.080
Precisely.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.120
Now, right now, Puerto Rico is surviving

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.000
on a temporary transitional payment

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.520
index of about 0.5259.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.520
But that transition period officially

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.680
dies at the start of fiscal year 2028,

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.440
which is October 1st, 2027.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.040
Oh, boy.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:40.840
Yeah.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.320
When that transition lapses, their wage index

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.520
collapses to the official raw data value for San Juan,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.640
which is 0.3500.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.520
0.35, meaning they would get

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.240
paid 35% of the national average

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.200
for the labor portion of their medical care.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.520
It is staggeringly low.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.920
I mean, to give you some context from the data pack,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.480
if you look at the entire mainland United States,

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.080
the absolute lowest wage index area is rural Mississippi.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:07.680
OK.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:08.320
And what's theirs?

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.800
Their index is 0.7112.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:12.920
Oh, wow.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:15.840
So San Juan is sitting at exactly half the rate

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.160
of the cheapest rural area in the mainland US.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:18.440
Yes.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.160
It's not even close.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.480
Now, let me play devil's advocate for a second here.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:23.280
Sure.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.000
Because the federal government's logic

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.560
is that things cost what they cost, right?

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.000
Like if the cost of living in San Juan is genuinely lower

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.440
and the hospitals are, in fact, paying their nurses $15

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.040
an hour instead of the $40 an hour they make in Boston.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:37.680
Right.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.720
Isn't the mathematical formula technically

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.360
working as intended?

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.240
Like why should the federal government pay them

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.560
Boston rates if they're actually

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.320
paying Puerto Rico wages?

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:50.000
That is the exact argument the federal government makes

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.320
when defending the formula.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.560
On paper, it looks perfectly rational.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:54.160
OK.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.440
But in practice, it creates a systemic track.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:59.520
It's this vicious cycle that the hospitals simply

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.600
cannot escape.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.960
It's like a gravity spiral.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.560
Think about the mechanics of it.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.840
Hospital labor isn't a free market in Puerto Rico.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.920
Medicare is so dominant there that it essentially

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.560
is the market.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:15.440
So when the wage index is suppressed to 0.35,

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.280
the hospitals receive drastically less Medicare

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:19.640
funding overall.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.640
Because their total revenue is so low,

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.520
they physically cannot afford to pay competitive wages.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:25.160
Right.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.800
And because they pay low wages, their required annual cost

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.200
reports show low labor costs.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.360
The federal government takes those exact cost reports,

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:37.320
feeds them back into the formula, and says,

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.040
Lee, your wages are low.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.480
So your index stays at 0.35.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:43.240
It's like being in a descending airplane

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.440
where the altimeter reading is actually hardwired

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:46.920
to the engines.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.640
The lower your altitude, the more the engines

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.040
throttle down, which just pulls you down faster.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:52.360
That's a perfect analogy.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.800
And the human cost of that math is massive brain drain.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.760
Because if I'm a bilingual nurse making $15 an hour in San

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.640
Juan, and a hospital in Orlando is offering me

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.200
a massive signing bonus and $45 an hour.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.000
They're leaving.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.280
I'm getting on a plane, yeah.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:08.720
And that brain drain just hollows out

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:10.280
the medical infrastructure.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.680
Now, for a long time, there was

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.620
a policy that artificially propped up Puerto Rico's

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.000
number, essentially doubling it

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.280
to keep the system afloat.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.040
OK.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:22.640
But the federal courts actually struck that down in a 2024 case

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:24.040
called Bridgeport.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.120
Well, they struck it down.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:25.760
Yeah.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.840
So CMS, which is the agency that runs Medicare,

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.880
instituted this temporary 0.52 transition just

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.240
to soften the blow.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.160
But that's a Band-Aid.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.760
And the Band-Aid gets ripped off in 2027.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:38.920
Exactly.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:40.640
Which brings us to a piece of data

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:43.800
in this strategy memo that genuinely shocked me.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.240
It, your is what the strategist

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.840
called the mobilization gap.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:48.680
Yes.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:49.280
The comments.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.880
Because you would assume that with a 15.5% overnight revenue

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.400
collapse staring them in the face,

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.920
every hospital executive, doctor, and politician in Puerto

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.200
Rico would be screaming from the rooftops.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.120
You'd think so.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.360
But the documents analyzed the federal comment period

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.320
for the fiscal year 2027 rules.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:09.320
When CMS proposed these changes,

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:11.480
they opened a public docket.

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:16.200
And out of 982 public comments submitted

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:17.120
to the federal government.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.720
Guess how many?

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.160
Only six mentioned Puerto Rico.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.800
And of those six, only four were actually

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:25.280
from Puerto Rican institutions.

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:26.000
Four comments.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.240
Four comments out of 982.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:29.160
That's insane.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.880
The campaign strategist referred to this

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.720
as the four of 982 embarrassment.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:34.840
How does that even happen?

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.960
You have a multi-billion-dollar industry

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.560
facing an existential threat and four people write a letter.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.340
It represents a complete breakdown

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:43.840
of institutional mobilization.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.120
I mean, administrative law operates entirely

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.000
on the written record.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.440
If the federal government proposes a rule

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.560
and you do not submit a mountain of evidence data

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.120
and legal arguments during that open comment period,

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:57.680
the government can legally say, well,

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.280
there is no evidence on the docket

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:00.280
that this is a priority.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:02.280
Right. It's a legal shield for them.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:03.120
Exactly.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.160
There's no data to justify changing our rule.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.280
And you cannot sue them later and win

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.560
if you didn't first build the administrative record.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.160
So they are paralyzed in this descending airplane.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.720
The ground is rapidly approaching in 2027.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.640
And nobody is even radioing the control tower.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:20.200
They're just bracing for impact.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.880
That is the baseline problem.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:22.720
Yeah.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.280
But the strategists who authored this war plan,

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:26.360
they aren't just telling people

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.080
to write angrier letters.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.280
They realize that asking the government nicely

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:33.640
for a handout just won't fix the gravity spiral.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:34.920
To break the math,

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:37.080
you basically have to find a loophole in the math

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.080
that someone else has already stress-tested.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.200
You look for legal precedent.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.180
And in the bizarre, highly technical world

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:44.960
of Medicare reimbursement law,

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.240
there is really no precedent more famous

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.920
or infamous, I should say, than the Nantucket strategy.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:51.760
Yeah.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.600
This is where the story shifts to Massachusetts.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:55.720
And the strategy documents spend pages

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:57.440
just dissecting a maneuver executed

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:01.000
by Nantucket Cottage Hospital back in 2008.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:02.480
It's a legendary maneuver.

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:03.840
So take us back.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:05.880
How did a wealthy resort island

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.040
break the Medicare wage index?

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.560
Well, to understand the Massachusetts windfall,

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:13.360
we have to look at a quirk in federal law.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.920
Specifically, section 4410

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.640
of the Balanced Budget Act of 1997.

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:19.480
Okay.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:23.360
This statute created something called the rural floor.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.080
The law dictates that any urban hospital in a given state

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:29.840
cannot receive a Medicare wage index

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:32.300
that is lower than the rural wage index

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:33.240
of that same state.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:34.080
Oh, I see.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.160
So it establishes a basement.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.840
Like if the rural hospitals in your state

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:41.240
are paying nurses an average of say $30 an hour,

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:44.320
then the giant urban hospitals in the big cities

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.000
can't be reimbursed at a rate lower than $30 an hour.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:47.840
Exactly.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.320
Even if their own internal data suggests they should be,

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.720
Congress intended it as a protective measure.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:53.560
Sure.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:55.640
To ensure rural hospitals could compete for talent

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:58.120
and to make sure urban hospitals didn't somehow fall

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.200
behind rural ones in the same state.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:01.440
Okay, that sounds well-intentioned.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.360
It does.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:05.600
But then Nantucket Cottage Hospital saw an opportunity.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:08.280
Prior to 2008, Nantucket operated

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.200
as a critical RACSES hospital.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.800
Wait, I need a translation there.

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:14.160
What is critical access hospital and why does that matter?

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:17.280
Right, so a critical access hospital, or CAH,

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.440
is a special designation for small,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.640
highly isolated facilities.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.120
To keep them from going bankrupt,

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:26.320
Medicare pays them based on their actual reasonable costs.

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:28.280
Oh, so it's not the wage index formula at all.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:31.800
No, it's a very safe protective financial system.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.720
But importantly, because they are

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.120
on the separate payment system,

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.800
their wage data is not used to calculate

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.600
the state's Medicare wage index.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.000
Okay, so they are completely walled off in the math.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:43.840
Right.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.920
But in 2008, Nantucket voluntarily gave up

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.960
that safe CAH status.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:51.120
They converted to an IPPS hospital,

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.200
which stands for inpatient perspective

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.520
payment system hospital.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:55.360
IPPS.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.560
Yes, that is the standard classification

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.480
for regular acute care hospitals.

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.200
But why would they leave a safe,

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.200
guaranteed cost system?

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:04.120
That sounds risky.

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:06.240
Because of the cost of living on Nantucket.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:07.480
As an island resort,

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:09.080
it is astronomically expensive

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:12.240
to hire doctors and nurses to live and work there.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.640
Nantucket had exceptionally high labor costs.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.440
By converting to an IPPS hospital,

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:18.480
their massive payroll data was suddenly fed

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:19.840
directly into the Massachusetts

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:21.400
wage index calculation.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:23.000
Okay, I think I see where this is going.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:25.280
And here is the genius of the maneuver.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:27.480
Because of how the geographic boundaries

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.400
were drawn in Massachusetts,

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:30.960
Nantucket effectively became

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:33.640
the only geographically rural hospital

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:36.280
in the entire state contributing to that data pool.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:39.000
Oh wow, if they are the only rural hospital.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.640
Their single sky high island payroll

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:44.640
dictated the rural index

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.480
for the entire state of Massachusetts.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:48.680
And what does section 4410 say?

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:50.520
The urban hospitals cannot be paid

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:52.440
less than the rural floor.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.040
So a tiny island hospital

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:55.960
effectively became the financial anchor

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:58.720
for a massive urban healthcare system

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:00.680
simply because of its geographic label.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.720
Nantucket's data became the mandatory

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.960
minimum wage index for nearly every

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:08.040
massive multi-billion dollar hospital system

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:08.880
in Boston.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:09.880
That is wild.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:11.440
The math on this and the documents

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:12.640
is just staggering.

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:15.960
It says by 2012, Nantucket's data alone

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.920
pushed the Massachusetts rural floor

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:19.920
to a massive 1.28.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:21.080
Yeah, huge bump.

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:22.360
And because the Medicare budget

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.440
is a fixed pie nationally,

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:25.680
like it's supposed to be budget-neutral, right?

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:26.280
Horsesack!

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.200
When Massachusetts takes a dramatically

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:30.040
larger slice of the pie,

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:31.520
every other hospital in the country

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:32.680
gets a smaller slice.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:33.360
Yep.

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.680
The documents estimate this single maneuver

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:39.560
transferred between $256 million

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:43.040
and $367 million per year

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:44.520
from the rest of the country

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:46.120
directly into Massachusetts.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.920
It was an absolute windfall.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.920
Just a master stroke of regulatory engineering.

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:51.720
But the rest of the country

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:53.360
had to be furious about that.

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.000
Oh, they were furious.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.480
It triggered a civil war in the healthcare system.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.800
21 other states formed a coalition

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:02.280
to fight Massachusetts in federal court.

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:03.360
21 states.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:04.720
How does that even play out?

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:06.440
Do the courts just look in and say,

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.720
okay, clearly this is a manipulation of the system.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:09.840
You guys have to stop.

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:11.240
You'd think so, right?

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:13.400
But the states fighting against Massachusetts

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:14.720
consistently lost.

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:15.560
They lost.

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:17.320
The federal judges looked at the statue

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:20.040
and ruled based strictly on the text.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:21.480
They essentially said,

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.360
the law says the urban index

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:25.800
cannot be lower than the rural index.

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.840
Nantucket is geographically rural.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.080
Their wages are their real wages.

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:31.120
They said, we don't care

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.280
about the downstream financial impact.

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:36.640
The mechanism is working exactly as Congress wrote it.

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:38.680
The letter of the law completely trumped

00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:39.760
the spirit of the law.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:40.680
Completely.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:42.280
Now, eventually the Department of Health

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:44.360
and Human Services Inspector General

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:46.160
aggressively audited Nantucket.

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:47.000
Okay.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:51.240
And they clawed back about $133.6 million

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:53.600
for a highly specific error

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:56.160
in how they reported certain wage data.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:57.400
They got them on the technicality.

00:13:57.400 --> 00:13:58.240
Right.

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:00.280
But the underlying legal mechanism,

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.680
the actual Nantucket strategy itself

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:04.800
survived judicial review.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.600
It became a proven battle-tested playbook

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:09.360
for exploiting the rural floor.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.920
And this brings us back to the war room in Puerto Rico.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.680
The strategists see the Massachusetts windfall.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:16.320
They look at a map of Puerto Rico

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:17.960
and they see an obvious parallel.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:19.560
They look at the island of VX.

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:20.400
Exactly.

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:22.840
The similarities are almost too perfect.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:24.800
VX is an island of municipality

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:26.800
off the eastern coast of Puerto Rico.

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:28.520
It is geographically isolated.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:29.960
It is definitively rural.

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:31.200
Just like Nantucket.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:32.040
Right.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:32.840
So the obvious play,

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:34.080
the one that probably gets pitched

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:36.600
in every initial brainstorming meeting

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.760
is just copy and paste the Massachusetts maneuver.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:39.600
Right.

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:40.440
Just do what they did.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:41.260
Yeah.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:42.960
You make the hospital and VX the rural anchor.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.880
You use its data to set a high rural floor

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:48.920
and suddenly all 52 hospitals in Puerto Rico

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:53.000
are lifted out of their 0.3500 death spiral

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:54.360
by the VX data.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:56.400
On paper, it is the most logically

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.680
elegant solution imaginable.

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:00.520
It perfectly mirrors a strategy

00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:03.240
that has already survived federal court.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:06.000
But this is where the strategy documents prove their worth

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:09.560
because they reveal a massive hidden landmine.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.120
They explain precisely why the obvious move

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:14.240
is actually a trap that would destroy

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:16.440
the territory's entire medical system.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:17.280
Right.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:18.120
Because to run the Nantucket play,

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:19.840
you need two distinct things.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:22.560
You need the rural geographic classification,

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:24.240
which VX has,

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:26.800
but you also need a fully licensed

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:29.600
Medicare certified acute care hospital,

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:33.080
an IPPS hospital that is paying incredibly high wages

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:34.440
and submitting cost reports.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.480
Now what does VX actually have right now?

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.000
The current facility there,

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:40.880
hospital Susanna Centino is just a CDT.

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:41.720
Right.

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:44.120
A CDT is a diagnostic and treatment center.

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:47.000
It's essentially an ambulatory emergency room.

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.880
It is not an inpatient hospital.

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:49.720
Okay.

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:51.760
And that distinction is everything.

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:55.320
A CDT does not submit the kind of rigorous

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.480
Medicare cost reports that feed

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:59.360
into the wage index calculations.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:01.240
Oh, so they don't even have the vehicle

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:02.280
to deliver the data.

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:03.120
Exactly.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:04.800
To make the strategy work,

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:06.480
the Puerto Rican government would have to either

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:09.280
build a new IPPS hospital from scratch

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:12.040
or massively upgrade the existing facility.

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:14.000
But the building is only half the problem.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.440
Just having the hospital exist isn't enough

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:18.680
because the wages have to be extraordinarily high

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:19.520
to move the needle.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:20.760
Because it's an average, right?

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:21.600
Yes.

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:23.840
The actuarial memo calculates that to push

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:26.440
the Puerto Rico floor up to a meaningful level,

00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:28.960
this hypothetical new VX hospital

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:32.000
would need to pay its staff about $36 an hour.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:33.720
Which, as we established earlier,

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:35.640
is more than double the prevailing wage

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.960
of about $15 an hour in Puerto Rico.

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:38.800
Right.

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:40.200
So the government would have to artificially

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.400
subsidize those massive salaries.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:44.400
They would have to fund an annual payroll

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:47.760
of $11 to $12 million entirely out of pocket.

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:48.600
Man.

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:49.920
And here is the kicker.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.560
Because of the built-in lag and federal data reporting,

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:56.640
they would have to burn that $12 million a year

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:00.240
for four to six years before the high-wage data ever actually

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:01.920
affected the federal wage index.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:04.600
Four to six years, burning tens of millions of dollars

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:06.720
just to generate the right paperwork, basically.

00:17:06.720 --> 00:17:07.560
A much.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:09.680
But here's the twist.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:13.080
The financial cost isn't even the real trap, is it?

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:15.680
The real trap is what happens if they try to build the hospital,

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:18.840
but they can't afford the $36 an hour salaries.

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:22.200
This is the most critical piece of regulatory analysis

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:23.760
in the entire memo.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:25.640
Because right now, Puerto Rico possesses

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:28.280
a highly unique precarious status.

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.440
Because the territory has no hospitals officially

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:33.040
classified as rural that are submitting wage data,

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:35.200
the federal government grants the entire island

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:36.520
an imputed floor.

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:37.560
An imputed floor.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:38.720
Okay, explain that.

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:41.520
It is a statutory safety net created recently

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:43.280
by the American Rescue Plan Act,

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:46.160
specifically Section 9831.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:49.040
Because Puerto Rico is considered an all-urban system

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:50.800
with no rural data,

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:53.640
CMS artificially imputes a floor for them

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:55.480
to prevent total collapse.

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:56.320
I see.

00:17:56.320 --> 00:17:59.920
For fiscal year 2027, that imputed safety net

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:02.520
is set at 0.3278.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:03.840
Okay, let me make sure I say this right.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:07.680
The safety net is 0.3278.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.040
It's obviously not great.

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:12.560
It's lower than the temporary 0.5 to transition rate

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:14.680
they're losing in 2027.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:16.000
But this is a hard basement.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:19.280
Like it stops the lowest wage hospitals in Puerto Rico

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:22.040
from falling to 0.20 or 0.15.

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:24.080
Correct, it is the absolute floor.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:27.240
But look closely at the legal trigger for that safety net.

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:31.040
That 0.3278 only exists under Section 9831

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:34.520
because Puerto Rico has no rural hospitals setting a rate.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:35.560
Oh, I see the trap.

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:37.720
Yeah, if the Puerto Rican government goes ahead

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:41.160
and opens a real IPPS hospital in VX,

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:43.880
but they can't afford to subsidize those artificially

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:46.240
high $36 an hour wages.

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.840
Meaning they just pay normal Puerto Rican hospital wages

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:50.200
of around $15 an hour.

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:51.080
What happens?

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:53.200
The federal computer system registers

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:56.240
a new active rural hospital.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:57.080
Yes.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:58.240
And the condition for the safety net,

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:00.880
having no rural hospitals just vanishes.

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:03.000
It's like they are suspended on a scaffolding

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:04.680
because there is no ground floor.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:06.240
The moment they build a ground floor,

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.500
even a really weak sunken one,

00:19:08.500 --> 00:19:10.400
the government takes away the scaffolding

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:13.760
and the whole system craches down to that new lower level.

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:14.600
Wow.

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:17.680
The memo calculates that a normal wage hospital in VX

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:22.560
would generate a real rural wage index of approximately 0.25.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:24.000
That is horrifying.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.360
By trying to execute the Nantucket strategy

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:28.800
without securing half a decade of massive funding

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:31.040
to guarantee artificially high wages,

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:35.440
they would accidentally destroy the 0.3278 imputed floor.

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:38.320
And crash healthcare funding for the entire territory

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:40.600
down to 0.25.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:42.480
It highlights the extreme danger

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.320
of half measures in administrative law.

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:45.160
Exactly.

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:46.800
From a public health perspective,

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:51.920
opening a modest normal wage hospital in VX is a massive win.

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:55.560
The residents of that island desperately need inpatient care.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:56.400
Of course they do.

00:19:56.400 --> 00:19:59.440
But under the perverse rules of Medicare reimbursement,

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:01.680
doing that without the payroll subsidies

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:04.880
is a financial catastrophe for the rest of Puerto Rico.

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:07.000
You can almost feel the anxiety of the lawyers

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.280
radiating off the pages of this memo.

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:12.000
They literally put a section in bold text

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:14.360
called the trap the letter walks into.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:15.360
They're terrified.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:16.200
They are.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.000
They're terrified that a well-meaning politician

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:19.720
is going to hold a press conference,

00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:23.720
announce a new rural hospital in VX for a quick PR win,

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:27.600
and just unknowingly trigger a systemic financial collapse.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:29.400
So the strategists are completely stuck here.

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:33.040
The 2027 cliff is arriving in exactly one year.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:36.200
Building a high-wage hospital in VX is too expensive.

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:38.400
The four to six-year data lag is too slow,

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.640
and the imputed floor trap is too dangerous.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:42.040
So they need a faster solution,

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:44.480
one that avoids VX entirely for now.

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:46.600
And this is where the campaign strategy

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:49.680
pivots to something they call Nantucket Plus.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:50.520
Nantucket Plus.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:54.040
If you can't build a new high-wage rural hospital in time,

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:56.960
how do you get high-wage data into the rural pool?

00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:57.800
Right.

00:20:57.800 --> 00:20:59.400
You use a completely different loophole,

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:01.440
urban to rural reclassification.

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:03.480
Okay, this revolves around a very specific

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:08.480
federal regulation, 42 CFR for 12.103,

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:12.360
the urban to rural loophole.

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:13.880
How does this actually work?

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:15.960
You're telling me an urban trauma center

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:18.600
in the middle of San Juan can just identify

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:19.760
as a rural facility?

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:21.120
Surprisingly, yes.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:24.520
Regulation 412.103 allows hospitals

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:26.840
that are physically located in urban areas

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:29.000
to legally apply to be treated as rural

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:30.760
for the purposes of the wage index.

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:31.600
Oh, why?

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:32.920
While it was originally designed to help

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:34.840
certain urban hospitals that function more

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:36.960
like rural facilities get better funding.

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:39.720
But surely CMS looks at a map and says,

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:41.440
you are a massive medical center

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:43.760
and a capital city of two million people,

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:45.120
request denied.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.040
You would think that common sense would prevail,

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:49.160
but again, we are dealing with the rigid text

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.600
of administrative law.

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:52.760
